# Line-ending policy: this repo tracks bytes, not normalised text.
#
# build/astronomy_2027.json is a deterministic artifact whose sha256 is the
# project's build identity. Its bytes are generated by build_2027.py (json.dump
# with indent=2 plus a trailing newline) and the generator's line endings are
# platform-deterministic. Git's core.autocrlf rewriting would store an
# LF-normalised blob and hand back a CRLF file on checkout, so a fresh clone
# would materialise a file whose sha256 is NOT the recorded build hash.
#
# Every path below is therefore pinned to binary: no EOL conversion, no diff
# munging. Repo config also sets core.autocrlf=false locally so that what is
# committed is byte-identical to what is on disk.
* -text -diff

# Keep a readable textual diff for the record documents and source, so the
# "which records changed" review is still possible by eye.
*.md -text diff
*.py -text diff
*.yaml -text diff
*.html -text diff
*.txt -text diff

# THE ONE EXCEPTION, and it is an exception to `-text`, not to the policy above.
#
# build/STEP4_DECISIONS.md is hand-edited -- it has no generator, so its bytes
# are whatever the last editor left. Under `-text` those bytes are committed
# as-is, and on 16-Sep-2026 the file was found MIXED: 3,186 CRLF lines and 318
# bare LF. Any tool that rewrites the whole file then flips all 3,504 lines, and
# the real change -- a paragraph, a number -- is invisible inside the flip. That
# has already happened once and nearly buried a content diff in 12,153 lines.
#
# `text eol=lf` pins both ends: the index blob is normalised to LF whatever the
# working tree holds, so no later editor can reproduce that diff, and checkout
# writes LF so tree and blob agree. No recorded sha256 depends on this file,
# which is why the byte-exactness rule above does not reach it.
build/STEP4_DECISIONS.md text eol=lf
